addSubject({id:'acc', topics:[{"t": "Concepts and qualitative characteristics", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define accounting and explain its functions and users</li><li>identify and apply the qualitative characteristics</li><li>define and apply accounting concepts (entity, monetary, going concern, period, matching, accrual, historical cost, conservatism, consistency, disclosure)</li><li>define the elements: assets, liabilities, equity, revenue, expenses</li></ul></div><h3>1. Accounting, functions and users</h3><p>Accounting is the process of <b>recording, classifying, summarising and reporting</b> financial information so users can make decisions. Functions: communication, decision making, accountability/stewardship, control.</p><table><tr><th>User</th><th>What they want to know</th></tr><tr><td>Owners/investors</td><td>profit, return on investment, risk</td></tr><tr><td>Managers</td><td>performance, costs, planning</td></tr><tr><td>Creditors/banks</td><td>can the business repay (liquidity)?</td></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>tax owed, statistics</td></tr><tr><td>Employees/unions</td><td>job security, ability to pay wages</td></tr><tr><td>Customers</td><td>will the business keep supplying?</td></tr></table><h3>2. Qualitative characteristics</h3><table><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Relevance</td><td>helps users make decisions; timely</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>free from error and bias, verifiable, faithful</td></tr><tr><td>Understandability</td><td>clear to users with reasonable knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>Comparability</td><td>can compare years and firms (consistency)</td></tr><tr><td>Timeliness</td><td>available in time to be useful</td></tr><tr><td>Neutrality / objectivity</td><td>not biased, based on evidence</td></tr></table><h3>3. Accounting concepts</h3><table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Entity</td><td>business is separate from the owner</td><td>owner's private car not recorded</td></tr><tr><td>Monetary</td><td>record in one currency unit (AUD in Kiribati)</td><td>NZ$ machine converted to AUD</td></tr><tr><td>Going concern</td><td>assume the business will continue</td><td>assets at cost, not sale value</td></tr><tr><td>Accounting period</td><td>report for a set period (year)</td><td>year ending 31 March</td></tr><tr><td>Matching / accrual</td><td>match expenses to revenue of the same period, when earned/incurred (not when cash moves)</td><td>accrued wages, prepaid insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Historical cost</td><td>record at original cost</td><td>land at purchase price</td></tr><tr><td>Conservatism (prudence)</td><td>don't overstate assets/profit</td><td>allowance for doubtful debts</td></tr><tr><td>Consistency</td><td>same methods each year</td><td>same depreciation method</td></tr><tr><td>Disclosure</td><td>reveal all important information</td><td>notes to the statements</td></tr></table><h3>4. Elements</h3><table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Definition</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Asset</td><td>resource <b>controlled</b> by the entity, from a past event, giving future benefits</td><td>cash, inventory, vehicle, accounts receivable</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>present obligation to give up resources</td><td>loan, accounts payable, GST owing</td></tr><tr><td>Equity</td><td>owner's claim = assets − liabilities</td><td>capital, profit, less drawings</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue</td><td>increase in equity from operations</td><td>sales, fees, rent received</td></tr><tr><td>Expense</td><td>decrease in equity from operations</td><td>wages, rent, depreciation</td></tr></table><div class=\"warn\">⚠ A HIRED asset is not an asset of the business – the hire charge is an expense.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "The entity concept assumes the owner and the business are…", "o": ["the same accounting entity", "different accounting entities", "different legal entities", "the same legal entity"], "a": 1, "e": "For a sole trader they are legally the same, but accounted for separately."}, {"q": "Which does NOT change owner's equity?", "o": ["Drawings", "A net surplus", "Owner contributes assets", "A bank loan received"], "a": 3, "e": "A loan increases assets AND liabilities."}, {"q": "A building is shown at its $100,000 cost, not its $250,000 value, because of…", "o": ["going concern", "historical cost", "accrual", "entity"], "a": 1, "e": "Recorded at cost."}, {"q": "Oven $15,000 + freight $1,500 + insurance and installation $2,500 is recorded as…", "o": ["$15,000", "$19,000", "$16,500", "an expense"], "a": 1, "e": "All costs to make it operational are capital expenditure."}, {"q": "Recording revenue when earned, not when cash is received, is the…", "o": ["accrual concept", "cost concept", "entity concept", "period concept"], "a": 0, "e": "Accrual basis."}, {"q": "Relevance means information…", "o": ["is old", "can influence a decision", "is in alphabetical order", "is audited"], "a": 1, "e": "Useful to the decision."}]}, {"t": "Processing: equation, documents, journals, ledger", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>apply the accounting equation to transactions</li><li>identify source documents</li><li>record in journals (sales, purchases, cash receipts, cash payments, general) and post to the ledger</li><li>prepare a trial balance</li></ul></div><h3>1. The accounting equation</h3><div class=\"key\">Assets = Liabilities + Equity<br>Equity = Capital + Revenue − Expenses − Drawings</div><table><tr><th>Transaction</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Owner contributes $1250 cash</td><td>Bank +1250, Capital +1250</td></tr><tr><td>Buys supplies on credit $2500</td><td>Supplies +2500, AP +2500</td></tr><tr><td>Customer pays $200 to settle $250 (discount $50)</td><td>Bank +200, AR −250, Equity −50 (discount allowed)</td></tr><tr><td>Owner takes $3000 cash</td><td>Bank −3000, Drawings (equity −3000)</td></tr><tr><td>Borrows $30 000</td><td>Bank +30 000, Loan +30 000</td></tr></table><h3>2. Source documents</h3><table><tr><th>Document</th><th>Used for</th></tr><tr><td>Tax invoice</td><td>credit sales/purchases</td></tr><tr><td>Receipt / cash register tape</td><td>cash received</td></tr><tr><td>Cheque butt / payment voucher</td><td>cash paid</td></tr><tr><td>Credit note</td><td>returns and allowances</td></tr><tr><td>Bank statement</td><td>checking bank</td></tr><tr><td>Memo</td><td>internal transactions (drawings of goods, adjustments)</td></tr></table><h3>3. Journals and ledger</h3><table><tr><th>Journal</th><th>Records</th></tr><tr><td>Sales journal</td><td>credit sales</td></tr><tr><td>Purchases journal</td><td>credit purchases</td></tr><tr><td>Cash receipts journal</td><td>all money in</td></tr><tr><td>Cash payments journal</td><td>all money out</td></tr><tr><td>General journal</td><td>everything else: adjustments, goods taken by owner, depreciation, corrections, closing entries</td></tr></table><p>Totals are <b>posted</b> to ledger accounts (T-accounts or running-balance). Debit: assets, expenses, drawings increase. Credit: liabilities, equity, revenue increase.</p><table><tr><th>Debit (Dr) increases</th><th>Credit (Cr) increases</th></tr><tr><td>Assets</td><td>Liabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Expenses</td><td>Equity (capital)</td></tr><tr><td>Drawings</td><td>Revenue</td></tr></table><h3>4. Running-balance bank account</h3><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Balance 2000 Dr → wages Cr 500 → 1500 Dr → sales Dr 2000 → 3500 Dr → purchases Cr 1600 → 1900 Dr → rent Cr 1000 → 900 Dr → telephone Cr 150 → 750 Dr → loan repaid Cr 2000 → 1250 Cr (overdrawn) → drawings Cr 600 → 1850 Cr → sales Dr 5000 → <b>3150 Dr</b>.</div><p>The <b>trial balance</b> lists every ledger balance; total debits must equal total credits (but errors of omission or wrong account are not detected).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "The correct order of the accounting process", "o": ["Journal → ledger → trial balance → statements", "Trial balance → statements → journal → ledger", "Ledger → TB → statements → journal", "TB → statements → ledger → journal"], "a": 0, "e": "Record, post, summarise, report."}, {"q": "To increase an expense you…", "o": ["debit", "credit", "do nothing", "close it"], "a": 0, "e": "Expenses increase on the debit side."}, {"q": "Paying a creditor $200 to settle a $250 debt gives…", "o": ["$50 discount received (equity +50)", "$50 loss", "$450 payable", "no entry"], "a": 0, "e": "AP −250, bank −200, revenue +50."}, {"q": "Source document for cash sales", "o": ["bank statement", "cash register summary/receipt", "invoice", "credit note"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash register tape."}, {"q": "Bank −3,000 and loan −3,000 means…", "o": ["borrowed $3,000", "repaid $3,000 of the loan", "bought equipment", "paid wages"], "a": 1, "e": "Both decrease."}, {"q": "Interest charged by the bank is first shown on the…", "o": ["invoice", "bank statement", "receipt", "cheque butt"], "a": 1, "e": "The bank deducts it directly."}]}, {"t": "Balance-day adjustments, closing and reversing entries", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>prepare balance-day adjustments: accrued and prepaid expenses and revenue, depreciation, doubtful debts, bad debts, inventory</li><li>prepare closing entries</li><li>prepare reversing entries</li></ul></div><h3>1. Why adjust?</h3><p>The <b>accrual/matching</b> concept: profit must include all revenue earned and expenses incurred <b>in the period</b>, whether or not cash has moved.</p><h3>2. The adjustments</h3><table><tr><th>Adjustment</th><th>Journal entry</th></tr><tr><td>Accrued expense (wages owing)</td><td>Dr Wages / Cr Accrued wages</td></tr><tr><td>Prepaid expense (insurance paid ahead)</td><td>Dr Prepaid insurance / Cr Insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Accrued revenue (commission earned, not received)</td><td>Dr Accrued commission / Cr Commission income</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue received in advance</td><td>Dr Rent income / Cr Income in advance</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Dr Depreciation / Cr Accumulated depreciation</td></tr><tr><td>Bad debt written off</td><td>Dr Bad debts / Cr Accounts receivable</td></tr><tr><td>Increase allowance for doubtful debts</td><td>Dr Doubtful debts / Cr Allowance for doubtful debts</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Insurance $1600 paid 1 Oct for 12 months; year ends 31 Mar: used 6 months = $800; prepaid $800 → Dr Prepaid insurance 800 / Cr Insurance 800.</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>AR $50 000, allowance $800, new rate 5%: needed $2500 → increase $1700 → Dr Doubtful debts 1700 / Cr Allowance 1700.</div><h3>3. Closing and reversing entries</h3><p><b>Closing</b>: transfer revenue and expenses to Profit and Loss Summary, then profit and drawings to Capital, so temporary accounts start at zero.</p><p><b>Reversing</b> (first day of the new period): reverse accruals and prepayments so the next payment is recorded normally, e.g. Dr Insurance / Cr Prepaid insurance.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Interest $300 owing at year end. Entry?", "o": ["Dr Accrued expenses / Cr Interest", "Dr Interest expense / Cr Accrued expenses", "Dr Bank / Cr Interest", "no entry"], "a": 1, "e": "Recognise the expense and the liability."}, {"q": "Rent paid $14,000; rent for the year is $11,500. Prepaid rent =", "o": ["$2,500", "$11,500", "$25,500", "$14,000"], "a": 0, "e": "The excess is an asset."}, {"q": "Commission earned but not received is…", "o": ["revenue in advance", "accrued revenue", "an accrued expense", "a prepayment"], "a": 1, "e": "An asset (receivable)."}, {"q": "Closing entries transfer expenses to…", "o": ["the balance sheet", "Profit and Loss Summary", "bank", "accounts payable"], "a": 1, "e": "Then net profit goes to capital."}, {"q": "Reversing entries are made…", "o": ["on the last day", "on the first day of the new period", "never", "monthly"], "a": 1, "e": "They reverse the accruals and prepayments."}, {"q": "Diminishing value, 20%: cost 16,000, accumulated depreciation 3,600. Depreciation =", "o": ["3,200", "2,480", "720", "12,400"], "a": 1, "e": "(16,000 − 3,600) × 20%."}]}, {"t": "Financial statements and cash flow", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>prepare a classified Statement of Financial Performance (trading, selling, administrative, financial sections)</li><li>prepare a classified Statement of Financial Position</li><li>prepare a Statement of Changes in Equity and a Statement of Cash Flows</li><li>state limitations of financial statements</li></ul></div><h3>1. Statement of Financial Performance</h3><table><tr><th>Layout</th></tr><tr><td>Sales − sales returns = <b>Net sales</b></td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (opening inventory + purchases + freight in − closing inventory)</td></tr><tr><td>= <b>Gross profit</b></td></tr><tr><td>+ other income (rent, commission, discount received)</td></tr><tr><td>− Selling and distribution expenses (advertising, delivery, sales wages, depreciation of vehicles)</td></tr><tr><td>− Administrative expenses (office salaries, insurance, rent, general, bad/doubtful debts, depreciation of office equipment)</td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses (interest, bank charges, discount allowed)</td></tr><tr><td>= <b>Net profit</b></td></tr></table><h3>2. Statement of Financial Position</h3><table><tr><th>Layout</th></tr><tr><td>Current assets: bank, AR less allowance, inventory, prepayments, accrued revenue</td></tr><tr><td>− Current liabilities: overdraft, AP, accrued expenses, GST, income in advance</td></tr><tr><td>= <b>Working capital</b></td></tr><tr><td>+ Non-current assets: cost − accumulated depreciation = carrying amount</td></tr><tr><td>− Non-current liabilities: mortgage, long-term loan</td></tr><tr><td>= <b>Net assets</b> = Equity: capital + profit − drawings</td></tr></table><div class=\"warn\">⚠ Bank shown on the credit side of a trial balance is an OVERDRAFT (current liability).</div><h3>3. Statement of Cash Flows</h3><p>Only <b>cash</b> moves: receipts (cash sales, from debtors, rent received) and payments (suppliers, wages, expenses, drawings in cash, loan repayments, assets bought). Leave out non-cash items: depreciation, bad debts, discounts.</p><div class=\"key\">Opening bank + receipts − payments = closing bank</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Receipts 949 760, payments 994 510 → net decrease 44 750; opening 5000 → closing (39 750) overdraft.</div><h3>4. Limitations</h3><ul><li>Based on estimates (depreciation, doubtful debts).</li><li>Historical cost ignores inflation and current value.</li><li>Leave out non-financial information (staff, environment, reputation).</li><li>Show the past, not the future.</li></ul>", "quiz": [{"q": "Which is NOT shown in a statement of cash flows?", "o": ["Cash drawings", "Bad debts written off", "Cash sales", "Wages paid"], "a": 1, "e": "Bad debts involve no cash."}, {"q": "Interest on a mortgage is classified as…", "o": ["an admin expense", "a finance cost", "a distribution cost", "revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Cost of borrowing."}, {"q": "Fees received in advance are…", "o": ["revenue", "a current liability", "an asset", "equity"], "a": 1, "e": "Not yet earned."}, {"q": "A mortgage due in 2040 is…", "o": ["a current liability", "a non-current liability", "an asset", "an expense"], "a": 1, "e": "Due after 12 months."}, {"q": "Bank balance $10,100, receipts $19,000, payments $17,400. Closing =", "o": ["$11,700", "$8,500", "$1,600", "$46,500"], "a": 0, "e": "10,100 + 1,600."}, {"q": "Goodwill is…", "o": ["a current asset", "an intangible non-current asset", "a liability", "an expense"], "a": 1, "e": "Intangible."}]}, {"t": "Non-profit (community) organisations", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe the nature of non-profit (community) organisations</li><li>prepare a Receipts and Payments account, trading statement and Income and Expenditure statement</li><li>prepare a Statement of Financial Position with an accumulated fund</li><li>treat subscriptions in arrears and in advance</li></ul></div><h3>1. Non-profit organisations</h3><p>Clubs, churches, sports associations exist to serve members, not make profit. Terms: <b>surplus/deficit</b> instead of profit/loss; <b>accumulated fund</b> instead of capital.</p><h3>2. The statements</h3><table><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Contents</th></tr><tr><td>Receipts and Payments</td><td>summary of cash (like a cash flow): opening bank + receipts − payments = closing bank</td></tr><tr><td>Trading statement (e.g. canteen, craft fair)</td><td>sales − cost of sales − direct expenses = profit from that activity</td></tr><tr><td>Income and Expenditure</td><td>accrual basis: subscriptions earned, donations, trading profit − expenses incl. depreciation = surplus</td></tr><tr><td>Statement of Financial Position</td><td>assets − liabilities = accumulated fund</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Craft club: sales 25 000; cost of sales = 2200 + (9000 + 2200 owing) − 2600 = 10 800; gross profit 14 200; less hall hire 5900, advertising 500, transport 1800, depreciation 600 → profit <b>5400</b>.</div><h3>3. Subscriptions</h3><p>Revenue = subscriptions for <b>this year</b> (accrual): add arrears at the end, subtract arrears from last year received, deduct subscriptions received in advance. Subscriptions in arrears = current asset; in advance = current liability.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>200 members × $50 = $10 000 revenue even if 3 have not paid ($150 in arrears).</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Subscriptions: 40 members × $20, received $700. Arrears =", "o": ["$100", "$700", "$800", "$0"], "a": 0, "e": "800 − 700."}, {"q": "A club's \"capital\" is called…", "o": ["retained earnings", "accumulated funds", "share capital", "drawings"], "a": 1, "e": "Accumulated funds."}, {"q": "The receipts and payments statement shows…", "o": ["accrued income", "only actual cash received and paid", "depreciation", "profit"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash basis."}, {"q": "Refreshment purchases paid $2,000 plus $250 owing. Purchases for the trading statement =", "o": ["$2,000", "$2,250", "$1,750", "$250"], "a": 1, "e": "Accrual basis."}, {"q": "The surplus of a club means…", "o": ["profit for owners", "income exceeded expenditure", "a bank loan", "cash"], "a": 1, "e": "Non-profit wording."}]}, {"t": "Analysis and interpretation (ratios)", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>calculate and interpret profitability, liquidity, financial stability and efficiency ratios</li><li>compare results with previous years and industry averages</li><li>make recommendations</li></ul></div><h3>1. Ratios</h3><table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula</th><th>Means</th></tr><tr><td>Gross profit %</td><td>GP ÷ net sales × 100</td><td>profit from trading</td></tr><tr><td>Mark-up %</td><td>GP ÷ cost of sales × 100</td><td>pricing</td></tr><tr><td>Net profit %</td><td>NP ÷ net sales × 100</td><td>overall profitability</td></tr><tr><td>Expense %</td><td>expense group ÷ sales × 100</td><td>cost control</td></tr><tr><td>Return on equity</td><td>NP ÷ average equity × 100</td><td>return to owner</td></tr><tr><td>Current ratio</td><td>CA ÷ CL</td><td>ability to pay short-term debts (aim ~2:1)</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid (quick) ratio</td><td>(CA − inventory − prepayments) ÷ CL</td><td>immediate ability to pay (aim ≥1:1)</td></tr><tr><td>Equity ratio</td><td>equity ÷ total assets</td><td>how much the owner finances</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory turnover</td><td>COGS ÷ average inventory</td><td>how fast stock sells</td></tr><tr><td>Debtors' age</td><td>average AR ÷ credit sales × 365</td><td>days to collect</td></tr></table><h3>2. Interpreting</h3><p>Always: (1) state the figure; (2) compare (last year, industry); (3) explain the likely <b>cause</b>; (4) give a <b>recommendation</b>.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Liquid ratio 0.90 : 1 → only 90c of liquid assets for every $1 of current debt; risk of not paying on time. Recommend: collect debtors faster, reduce inventory, arrange overdraft limit.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Mark-up is 25%. Gross profit % =", "o": ["20%", "25%", "33.3%", "50%"], "a": 0, "e": "25/125."}, {"q": "Current ratio 2:1 means…", "o": ["$2 of CA for every $1 of CL", "$2 of profit", "2 days", "a loss"], "a": 0, "e": "Liquidity."}, {"q": "Which measures financial stability?", "o": ["Equity ratio", "Inventory turnover", "Mark-up", "GP%"], "a": 0, "e": "Equity and debt ratios."}, {"q": "Age of receivables 61 days when the norm is 30. This is…", "o": ["satisfactory", "unsatisfactory – slow collection", "ideal", "not relevant"], "a": 1, "e": "Risk of bad debts and cash shortage."}, {"q": "COGS $140,000, average inventory $14,000. Turnover =", "o": ["10 times", "14 times", "20 times", "5 times"], "a": 0, "e": "140,000 ÷ 14,000."}, {"q": "Net profit $60k, opening equity $220k, closing $240k. ROE ≈", "o": ["25%", "26%", "27%", "36%"], "a": 1, "e": "60/230 = 26.1%."}]}, {"t": "Internal control, cash and bank reconciliation", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the objectives and principles of internal control</li><li>describe controls for cash receipts and payments</li><li>prepare an updated bank account and a bank reconciliation statement</li></ul></div><h3>1. Internal control</h3><table><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Authorisation</td><td>manager approves purchases</td></tr><tr><td>Separation of duties</td><td>one receives cash, another records it, a third reconciles</td></tr><tr><td>Documentation</td><td>pre-numbered invoices and cheques</td></tr><tr><td>Physical safeguards</td><td>safe, locked storeroom, passwords</td></tr><tr><td>Independent checks</td><td>surprise cash counts, audits</td></tr></table><p>Objectives: safeguard assets, accurate records, efficiency, follow policies.</p><h3>2. Cash controls</h3><ul><li>Receipts: issue pre-numbered receipts, bank daily intact, two people open mail.</li><li>Payments: pay by cheque/EFT, authorised with invoice, two signatures, petty cash imprest system.</li></ul><h3>3. Bank reconciliation</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>compare the bank statement with the cash journals; tick matching items</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>update the bank account: add bank interest/direct credits; subtract bank fees, dishonoured cheques, direct debits</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>reconciliation: balance per bank statement + deposits not yet credited − unpresented cheques = balance per bank account</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Bank statement 2380 Cr + deposit 180 − unpresented cheques 425 = 2135 = updated bank account ✓</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A guard who also keeps the stock records breaks…", "o": ["authorisation", "separation of duties", "reliable personnel", "rotation"], "a": 1, "e": "Custody and recording should be separate."}, {"q": "Installing CCTV mainly meets which objective?", "o": ["Safeguarding assets", "Increasing sales", "Tax compliance", "Marketing"], "a": 0, "e": "Protects inventory and cash."}, {"q": "An unpresented cheque is…", "o": ["added to the statement balance", "subtracted from the statement balance", "added to the bank account", "ignored"], "a": 1, "e": "The bank has not paid it yet."}, {"q": "A bank fee on the statement only is…", "o": ["entered in the cash payments journal", "ignored", "added to receipts", "an unpresented cheque"], "a": 0, "e": "Update the business records."}, {"q": "Why pay by cheque or EFT rather than cash?", "o": ["it is faster", "it creates an authorised, traceable record", "there is no record", "it is cheaper"], "a": 1, "e": "Authorisation and audit trail."}]}, {"t": "Inventory, receivables, fixed assets and payroll", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>use perpetual inventory (FIFO, weighted average); reorder points</li><li>manage accounts receivable and payable (subsidiary ledgers, control accounts)</li><li>account for fixed assets and depreciation (straight-line, diminishing value)</li><li>process payroll (time sheets, gross to net pay)</li></ul></div><h3>1. Inventory (FIFO)</h3><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Jun 1: 5 @ $160. Jun 5 sell 3 → 2 @ 160. Jun 12 buy 5 @ 180. Jun 14 return 1 to supplier (@160) → 1 @ 160 + 5 @ 180. Jun 20 sell 4 → 1 @ 160 + 3 @ 180 = 700 out → 2 @ 180. Jun 29 owner takes 1 → <b>1 @ 180 = $180</b>.</div><p>FIFO: oldest stock is sold first. Reorder point = level at which new stock must be ordered.</p><h3>2. Receivables and payables</h3><p>Each customer/supplier has a subsidiary ledger account; their total must agree with the control account. Controls: credit checks and limits, monthly statements, ageing of debtors; check supplier invoices against orders and goods received.</p><h3>3. Fixed assets and depreciation</h3><div class=\"key\">Straight line = (cost − residual) ÷ useful life<br>Diminishing value = rate × carrying amount (opening)</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Minivan $30 000, 15% DV: Y1 4500 → 25 500; Y2 3825 → 21 675; Y3 3251 → 18 424; Y4 2764 → 15 660. (Insurance/registration are expenses, not cost.)</div><p>Cost includes everything to get the asset ready (price, freight, installation).</p><h3>4. Payroll</h3><div class=\"key\">Gross pay = ordinary hours × rate + overtime hours × rate × 1.5 (or 2)<br>Net pay = gross − PAYE tax − KPF (provident fund) − other deductions</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>40 h at $15 + 4 h at time-and-a-half: 600 + 90 = $690.</div><p>Time sheets record hours worked and are signed by a supervisor (prevents fraud). Pay by direct credit is safest.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "An inventory card with costs re-averaged after each purchase uses…", "o": ["FIFO", "LIFO", "weighted average cost", "specific cost"], "a": 2, "e": "WAC."}, {"q": "A subsidiary ledger contains…", "o": ["totals only", "a separate account for each customer", "only cash", "the trial balance"], "a": 1, "e": "The control account holds the total."}, {"q": "A fixed asset register helps by…", "o": ["recording each asset's location, responsibility and history", "increasing sales", "paying wages", "counting cash"], "a": 0, "e": "Safeguards assets."}, {"q": "Units of use: cost 25,000, residual 4,000, life 150,000 km, used 25,000 km. Depreciation =", "o": ["$3,500", "$4,167", "$5,000", "$2,500"], "a": 0, "e": "0.14 × 25,000."}, {"q": "A source document for payroll accuracy", "o": ["timesheet", "invoice", "credit note", "deposit slip"], "a": 0, "e": "Records the hours worked."}, {"q": "Purchase orders should be pre-numbered so that…", "o": ["they look neat", "missing or duplicate orders can be detected", "they are cheaper", "suppliers pay faster"], "a": 1, "e": "A completeness control."}]}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC Accounting_2025.pdf', note:'All figures are recalculated independently. ⚠ shows where the official marking scheme is wrong or incomplete.',
secs:[
{t:'Section One – Multiple choice (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'The accounting entity concept assumes the owner and the business…',a:'<b>B</b>: are different ACCOUNTING entities.', flag:'The marking scheme gives C ("different legal entities"). For a sole trader, the owner and business are the SAME legal entity; they are only separated for accounting. B is correct.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Reasons owner\'s equity changes, EXCEPT…',a:'<b>D</b>: a bank loan. It increases assets and liabilities equally, so equity does not change.', flag:'The marking scheme gives C, but an owner contributing assets DOES increase equity. D is the exception.'},
{n:'3.',q:'The accounting process',a:'<b>A</b>: journal → ledger → trial balance → financial statements.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Main purpose of a chart of accounts',a:'<b>C</b>: to provide a numerical index to the ledger (accounts are numbered and classified).', flag:'The marking scheme gives B ("classify accounts in numerical order"). Both describe the numbering, but C is the standard textbook definition.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Best reason for balance-day adjustments',a:'<b>C</b>: to ensure the correct assets, liabilities, revenue and expenses are reported in the financial statements (adjustments affect both statements).', flag:'The marking scheme gives B (correct revenue and expenses in the Statement of Financial Performance). That is the main reason, but C is more complete.'},
{n:'6.',q:'A club\'s Statement of Cash Flows shows…',a:'<b>B</b>: the actual cash received and paid during the period.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Return on equity (net surplus $60,000; drawings $40,000; closing equity $240,000)',a:'Opening equity = 240,000 − 60,000 + 40,000 = 220,000\nAverage = (220,000 + 240,000)/2 = 230,000\nROE = 60,000/230,000 = 26.1% → <b>B, 26%</b>'},
{n:'8.',q:'Mark-up 25%. Gross profit %?',a:'<b>A, 20%</b>: GP% = 25/(100 + 25) = 20%.'},
{n:'9.',q:'Procedure to control fixed asset purchases',a:'<b>A</b>: proper authorisation and record-keeping.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Means of paying employees, EXCEPT…',a:'<b>B</b>: credit (employees are paid by cash, cheque or direct credit).'}
]},
{t:'Section Two – Conceptual basis (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'How functions of accounting information help: decision making, accountability, control, interested parties.',a:'Decision making: gives owners and managers figures for budgeting, pricing, investing and expanding.\nAccountability: people who handle money must report how it was used (important for non-profits and government).\nControl device: actual results are compared with budgets to spot problems and correct them.\nInterested parties: banks (to lend), suppliers (credit), government (tax) and investors use the reports to make decisions.'},
{n:'2.',q:'How the government uses businesses\' accounting information.',a:'Mainly for <b>taxation</b>: business income and sales figures are used to assess income tax and VAT payable, so tax is collected fairly. The government also uses business data for national statistics (GDP), economic planning and checking that businesses meet laws and licence conditions.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Explain relevance in relation to Mr T\'s plan to buy a retail store.',a:'Information is relevant if it can influence a decision. Mr T needs information about the retail store\'s profitability, cash flows, debts and asset values, and his own ability to finance the purchase. Information about past cleaning jobs is not relevant to this decision.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Why is the building shown at $100,000, not $250,000?',a:'Because of the <b>historical cost</b> concept (supported by reliability and going concern): assets are recorded at the price actually paid, which is objective and verifiable. $250,000 is only an estimate of market value and could be biased. The business is not planning to sell, so the market value is not needed.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Relationship between going concern and accounting period.',a:'Going concern assumes the business will continue operating into the foreseeable future. Because its life is assumed to be long, it is divided into equal accounting periods (e.g. one year) so that performance can be measured and reported regularly, with assets depreciated over several periods.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Why is the $100,000 loan correctly a non-current liability? (recognition criteria)',a:'It meets the liability definition: a <b>present obligation</b> arising from a <b>past event</b> (borrowing the money), and it will lead to a probable future <b>outflow of resources</b> (repayments) that can be <b>measured reliably</b> ($100,000). It is non-current because it is repayable after more than 12 months.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Why record the oven at $19,000 instead of $15,000?',a:'All costs needed to buy the asset AND bring it to its location and working condition are <b>capital expenditure</b> and form part of the asset\'s cost: $15,000 + freight $1,500 + transit insurance and installation $2,500 = $19,000. They are not revenue (running) expenses, so they are depreciated over the oven\'s life.'}
]},
{t:'Section Three – Processing (32 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'One advantage of computerised processing.',a:'Faster and more accurate: totals and postings are done automatically, so there are fewer arithmetic errors, and reports are available instantly. (Also: easy backup and storage.)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Describe events A–D in the accounting equation.',a:'A. Repaid $3,000 of the bank loan.\nB. Paid an account payable of $250 with $200 and received a $50 discount.\nC. Bought furniture and appliances (inventory) on credit for $2,500.\nD. Sold an old van (fixed asset) on credit for $3,400.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Source documents: A. owner withdraws cash B. cash sales C. interest received on loan.',a:'A. Cheque butt or withdrawal slip (memo)\nB. Cash register summary or duplicate receipt\nC. Bank statement'},
{n:'4a.',q:'Sweety Toothless: Cash Payments Journal for July.',a:'Columns: Bank | Purchases | Rent | Sundry\nJul 10 Purchases 720 | 720\n15 Rent 450 | | 450\n18 Equipment 1,260 | | | 1,260\n20 Purchases 630 | 630\n26 Rent 450 | | 450\n30 Interest on loan 45 | | | 45\n<b>Totals: Bank 3,555 | Purchases 1,350 | Rent 900 | Sundry 1,305</b>', flag:'The marking scheme leaves out the $1,260 equipment bought for cash on 18 July and totals the bank column as 2,295. Every cash payment must go in the CPJ, so the correct bank total is $3,555.'},
{n:'4b.',q:'Bank account for July.',a:'1 Jul Balance 2,800 Dr\nDebits (cash receipts): 1,710 + 1,800 + 2,700 = 6,210\nCredits (cash payments, from the CPJ): 3,555\nClosing balance = 2,800 + 6,210 − 3,555 = <b>$5,455 Dr</b>'},
{n:'5a.',q:'Balance-day adjustments: (1) commission earned $600 not received (2) rent expense for the year is $11,500.',a:'(1) Dr Accrued commission (receivable) 600 / Cr Commission revenue 600\n(2) The trial balance shows rent of $14,000, but the year\'s expense is $11,500, so $2,500 is prepaid:\nDr Prepaid rent 2,500 / Cr Rent 2,500'},
{n:'5b.',q:'Closing entry for telephone expenses.',a:'Dr Profit and Loss Summary 5,000 / Cr Telephone expenses 5,000'},
{n:'5c.',q:'Reversing entries for accrued expenses ($300) and revenue in advance ($2,500).',a:'1 April (first day of the new year):\nDr Accrued expenses 300 / Cr Interest expense 300\nDr Income (rent revenue) received in advance 2,500 / Cr Rent revenue 2,500'}
]},
{t:'Section Four – Accounting reports (56 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Movers & Delivery King: classified Statement of Financial Performance, year ended 31 March 2025.',a:'<b>Revenue</b>: Fees 146,000 + 2,400 owing − 350 in advance = <b>148,050</b>\n\n<b>Moving and delivery expenses</b>: Fuel 39,500; Insurance (vehicles) 2,100; Repairs and maintenance 9,300; Wages – movers 35,000 + 900 = 35,900; Depreciation – vehicles 300,000 × 2% = 6,000 → <b>92,800</b>\n<b>Administrative expenses</b>: Cleaning supplies 9,500; Electricity 5,100 + 400 = 5,500; Postage and stationery 1,500; Rates 3,800; Telephone and internet 1,900; Wages – office 18,800; Bad debts 560; Depreciation – equipment (16,000 − 3,600) × 20% = 2,480 → <b>44,040</b>\n<b>Finance costs</b>: Interest on mortgage 13,750 + 1 month owing (200,000 × 7.5% ÷ 12 = 1,250) → <b>15,000</b>\nTotal expenses = <b>151,840</b>\n<b>Net deficit = 148,050 − 151,840 = (3,790)</b>'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Statement of Financial Position extract: current liabilities and non-current assets.',a:'<b>Current liabilities</b>: Accounts payable 7,300; Bank overdraft 2,400; Accrued expenses (wages 900 + interest 1,250 + electricity 400) 2,550; Fees in advance 350 → <b>12,600</b>\n<b>Non-current assets</b>: Land 400,000; Delivery vehicles 300,000 − 38,000 = 262,000; Equipment 16,000 − 6,080 = 9,920; Goodwill 15,000 → <b>686,920</b>\n(The mortgage of 200,000, due in 2040, is a non-current liability.)'},
{n:'A3.',q:'Accurate Accounting Services: statement of cash flows for October.',a:'Cash received from: customers on account 15,000; cash fees 3,500; sale of old computer 500 → <b>19,000</b>\nLess cash applied to: office wages 2,400; drawings 5,000; transfer to term deposit 10,000 → <b>17,400</b>\nNet increase in cash = 1,600\n+ Bank balance 1/10/2024 = 10,100\n<b>Bank balance 31/10/2024 = 11,700</b>\n(The bad debt, loss on sale and unpaid invoice are non-cash items, so they are excluded.)'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Function of (a) the Statement of Accounting Policies (b) the Statement of Financial Position.',a:'(a) It explains the rules and methods used to prepare the reports (entity, historical cost basis, depreciation and inventory methods, GST), so users can understand and compare them.\n(b) It shows the financial position at a point in time: what the entity owns (assets), owes (liabilities) and the owner\'s equity, so users can judge liquidity and stability.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Two features of a community organisation.',a:'1. It is run to serve members or the community, not to make a profit for owners (a surplus is reused for the club).\n2. It is funded by subscriptions, donations and fundraising, and managed by an elected volunteer committee.'},
{n:'B2a.',q:'Mwaneiko Club: subscriptions in arrears.',a:'Due = 38 × $25 = $950; received $750 → <b>arrears $200</b>'},
{n:'B2b.',q:'Refreshment trading statement.',a:'Sales 5,600\nLess cost of sales: opening stock 500 + purchases (2,000 + 250 owing) 2,250 = 2,750 − closing stock 650 = <b>2,100</b>\nGross profit = <b>3,500</b>\nLess: wages – refreshment 200; depreciation – refreshment equipment (200 + 3,000) × 20% = 640 → 840\n<b>Profit from refreshments = 2,660</b>'},
{n:'B2c.',q:'Statement of Financial Position extract: current and non-current assets.',a:'<b>Current assets</b>: Bank 700 + 6,650 − 6,400 = 950; Refreshment stock 650; Subscriptions in arrears 200 → <b>1,800</b>\n<b>Non-current assets</b>: Football equipment (1,000 + 800) 1,800 − depreciation 180 = 1,620; Refreshment equipment 3,200 − 640 = 2,560 → <b>4,180</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section Five – Analysis (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Two ratios that measure financial stability.',a:'<b>Equity ratio</b> (equity ÷ total assets) and <b>debt ratio</b> (total liabilities ÷ total assets).'},
{n:'2a.',q:'Fresh Salads 2025: current ratio, liquid ratio, age of receivables, inventory turnover.',a:'Current ratio = 60,000/60,000 = <b>1.00 : 1</b>\nLiquid ratio = (60,000 − 16,000)/(60,000 − 5,000) = 44,000/55,000 = <b>0.80 : 1</b>\n(Only the $5,000 of the secured overdraft that is within its limit is excluded; the $15,000 over the limit is treated as payable now.)\nAge of receivables = average AR (36,000 + 44,000)/2 = 40,000 ÷ credit sales (80% × 300,000 = 240,000) × 365 = <b>61 days</b>\nInventory turnover = COGS (300,000 − 160,000 = 140,000) ÷ average inventory (12,000 + 16,000)/2 = 14,000 = <b>10 times</b>', flag:'If your teacher excludes the whole overdraft, the liquid ratio is 44,000/40,000 = 1.10 : 1. Show your working either way.'},
{n:'2b.',q:'Is the age of receivables in 2025 satisfactory?',a:'<b>Unsatisfactory.</b> It worsened from 40 to 61 days, well above the normal 30-day credit term. Customers are paying slowly, which ties up cash (the overdraft has grown) and increases the risk of bad debts.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Meaning of (a) current ratio 2:1 (b) net profit 65%.',a:'(a) The business has $2 of current assets for every $1 of current liabilities, so it can comfortably pay its short-term debts.\n(b) For every $1 of sales, 65 cents is net profit after all expenses.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Purpose of analysing financial information.',a:'To turn raw figures into meaningful information that shows how well the business is performing and how strong its position is, by comparing with past years, budgets and industry averages. This identifies trends and problems so that owners and managers can make informed decisions.'}
]},
{t:'Section Six – Accounting systems (56 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Define "reliable personnel".',a:'Employing honest, competent and well-trained staff (checking references and qualifications), and supervising them, so that assets and records are handled properly.'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Security guard also keeps the stock records: weakness and recommendation.',a:'a. There is no <b>separation of duties</b>: the same person controls the materials and records them, so theft could be hidden by changing the records.\nb. Give the record-keeping to a separate storeperson or clerk. The guard only provides security, and stock counts are checked regularly by a third person.'},
{n:'A3.',q:'CCTV in the restaurants: objective; another way to reduce theft.',a:'a. <b>Safeguarding assets</b> (protecting inventory and cash).\nb. Separation of duties and regular stocktakes compared with the records; locked storerooms with limited key holders; or employing reliable personnel.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'"All payments should be made by cheque" in relation to authorisation.',a:'Cheques (or EFT) must be signed or approved by authorised people (often two signatories) after checking the supporting invoice. Only approved payments are made, and each one leaves a written record (cheque butt, bank statement) that can be checked.'},
{n:'B2.',q:'Purpose of a bank reconciliation.',a:'To check that the business\'s bank account agrees with the bank statement, to explain differences (unpresented cheques, outstanding deposits, bank charges), to find and correct errors or fraud, and to update the cash journals.'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Kiri Crafts bank reconciliation at 30 April 2025.',a:'Updated bank account: 6,684 + 7,880 − 4,150 = 10,414; + direct deposit 90 − loan interest 180 − loan principal 200 − bank fee 45 = <b>10,079 Dr</b>\nReconciliation statement:\nBalance as per bank statement 10,214 Cr\n+ Deposit not yet credited 315 → 10,529\n− Unpresented cheques #155 270 and #162 180 = 450\n= <b>Balance as per bank account 10,079 Dr</b> ✓'},
{n:'C1.',q:'Efficient Manufacturing: (a) an example of separation of duties (b) why pre-numbered POs?',a:'a. The storeperson requests the materials (requisition), the purchasing officer orders them, and the raw-materials clerk records and files the order: three different people.\nb. Pre-numbering means every purchase order can be accounted for. Missing, duplicate or unauthorised orders are easy to detect, preventing fraud such as fake orders.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Wizzle\'s inventory card: (a) method (b) alternative (c) cost of sales (d) record the drawings and stocktake.',a:'a. <b>Weighted average cost</b> (the unit cost is re-averaged after each purchase).\nb. FIFO (first in, first out).\nc. 2,000 + 2,700 + 6,600 = <b>$11,300</b>\nd. 31 Oct Drawings: OUT 50 × 15.50 = 775 → balance 550 × 15.50 = 8,525\n31 Oct Stock shortage: OUT 60 × 15.50 = 930 → balance <b>490 × 15.50 = 7,595</b>'},
{n:'D1.',q:'(a) A problem from weak control over credit purchases (b) why control credit sales?',a:'a. The business could pay for goods never received, pay a fake or duplicate invoice, or a staff member could order goods for personal use, causing losses.\nb. To make sure credit is given only to creditworthy customers within limits, that invoices are accurate and that debts are collected on time. This reduces bad debts and keeps cash flowing.'},
{n:'D2.',q:'What is a subsidiary ledger, and how does it work with the control account?',a:'A subsidiary ledger has a separate account for each customer, showing their invoices, payments and balance. The AR control account in the general ledger records the same transactions in total. At month end the subsidiary balances are listed and must equal the control account balance; any difference shows an error to fix before customer statements are sent.'},
{n:'D3.',q:'Heirloom Furniture: Accounts Receivable control account, June.',a:'1/6 Balance 55,200 Dr\n11/6 Freight charged (incl. GST) 3,220 Dr → 58,420\n25/6 Interest on overdue account 230 Dr → 58,650\n30/6 Credit sales (sales journal) 43,700 Dr → 102,350\n30/6 Cash receipts journal – Accounts receivable column 50,600 Cr → 51,750\n(This column already includes the discount allowed: cash 54,395 = 50,600 − 2,200 − 330 + 5,830 + 495, so the discount is NOT credited again.)\n30/6 Sales returns (credit notes) 1,035 Cr → 50,715\n30/6 Bad debts written off 3,450 Cr → <b>47,265 Dr</b>'},
{n:'E1.',q:'School photocopier: (a) a control at purchase (b) how a fixed asset register helps (c) why depreciate.',a:'a. Authorise the purchase, then tag/label the machine with an asset number and assign a named person responsible for it.\nb. The register records the asset number, cost, location, person responsible, serial number, warranty and repair history, so the school always knows where it is and who is responsible, and can check it regularly.\nc. Depreciation spreads the cost over the machine\'s useful life (matching cost with the periods that use it) and shows its true carrying value, so the school can plan to replace it.'},
{n:'E2.',q:'Vanessa\'s car: cost 25,000, residual 4,000, life 150,000 km. Journals: (a) transfer to disposal (b) Year 1 depreciation (c) transfer of accumulated depreciation (traded end of Sept 2022).',a:'Rate = (25,000 − 4,000)/150,000 = $0.14 per km\na. Dr Disposal 25,000 / Cr Vehicle 25,000\nb. Year 1: 25,000 km × 0.14 = 3,500 → Dr Depreciation – vehicle 3,500 / Cr Accumulated depreciation – vehicle 3,500\nc. Accumulated depreciation to 30 Sept 2022 = (25,000 + 30,000 + ¾ × 35,000 = 26,250 km) × 0.14 = 81,250 × 0.14 = 11,375 → Dr Accumulated depreciation 11,375 / Cr Disposal 11,375'},
{n:'F1.',q:'One payroll source document for accuracy.',a:'<b>Timesheet</b> (or time card / clock card).'},
{n:'F2.',q:'Rick: 8 h/day Mon–Fri at $6/h. Overtime: Mon 1, Wed 3, Fri 2, Sat 4, Sun 2. Deductions: KPF $90 + insurance $70. Net pay?',a:'Ordinary: 40 h × $6 = $240\nTime and a half ($9): Mon 1 + Wed 2 + Fri 2 + Sat 2 = 7 h × $9 = $63\nDouble time ($12): Wed 1 + Sat 2 + Sun 2 = 5 h × $12 = $60\nGross pay = 240 + 63 + 60 = $363\nDeductions = 90 + 70 = $160\n<b>Net pay = $203</b>'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC Accounting Exam.pdf', note:'Worked answers, every figure checked by calculation. ⚠ marks places where the MOE marking scheme or the question has an error.',
secs:[
{t:'Section 1 – Multiple choice (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Food truck $60 000 + $15 000 installation; running costs $650/month. Cost reported?',a:'<b>B: $75 000</b>. The cost includes everything needed to get the asset ready for use. Running costs are expenses.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Esther\'s investment (equity)',a:'Assets = 1200 + 7500 + 2000 + 2575 = 13 275\nLiabilities = 4150 + 650 = 4800\nEquity = 13 275 − 4800 = <b>$8 475 (C)</b>'},
{n:'3.',q:'Which goes in the general journal?',a:'<b>D</b>: stock taken by the owner (no cash involved). A and B go in the cash receipts journal, and C in cash payments.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Advantage of computerised reporting',a:'<b>A</b>: reports are available on demand.'},
{n:'5.',q:'200 members × $50; 3 have not paid. Subscription revenue?',a:'Accrual basis: all 200 members owe for this year → 200 × 50 = <b>$10 000 (A)</b>'},
{n:'6.',q:'Subscriptions in arrears are shown under…',a:'<b>B</b>: current assets (money owed to the club).'},
{n:'7.',q:'Return on equity',a:'Net surplus ÷ average equity = 50 000 ÷ [(40 000 + 60 000)/2] = 50 000/50 000 = <b>100% (D)</b>\n(If you first deducted a manager\'s wage equal to the drawings, it would be 20 000/50 000 = 40%. The MOE answer is D.)'},
{n:'8.',q:'Equity ratio',a:'Total assets = equity + liabilities = 60 000 + 20 000 = 80 000\nEquity ratio = 60 000/80 000 = <b>0.75 : 1 (A)</b>'},
{n:'9.',q:'$20 000, 4 years, residual $4000. Accumulated depreciation after year 3',a:'Straight line: (20 000 − 4000)/4 = 4000 per year × 3 = <b>$12 000 (C)</b>'},
{n:'10.',q:'Amy: 40 h ordinary + 4 h time-and-a-half at $15',a:'40 × 15 = 600; 4 × 22.50 = 90 → <b>$690 (D)</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section 2 – Conceptual basis (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Match users to uses',a:'1. Employee welfare: <b>Unions</b>\n2. Tax to collect: <b>Government</b>\n3. Overall financial health: <b>Managers</b>\n4. Risks of a venture / viability: <b>Investors</b>'},
{n:'2.',q:'Reliability',a:'Information is <b>reliable</b> when it is <b>free from error and bias</b>, can be <b>checked (verified)</b> against evidence such as source documents, and faithfully shows what it says it shows. Users can depend on it.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Monetary concept: why record the NZ machine in AUD?',a:'The <b>monetary concept</b> says all transactions are recorded in money using <b>one common unit of measurement</b>. Kiribati uses the <b>Australian dollar</b>, so B&T must convert the NZ$25 000 into AUD ($23 000) so that all its records and reports use the same currency and can be added and compared.'},
{n:'4.',q:'A hired truck recorded as a fixed asset: (a) why is it wrong? (b) correct treatment',a:'(a) An asset is a resource <b>controlled by the entity</b>, arising from a past event, that will give <b>future economic benefits</b>. A <b>hired</b> truck belongs to someone else; the business does not own or control it. So it is <b>not an asset</b>.\n(b) Record the hire charge as an <b>expense</b> (Truck hire / delivery expense) in the Statement of Financial Performance for the period it is used.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Consistency and disclosure',a:'<b>Consistency</b>: use the <b>same accounting methods</b> (e.g. depreciation method) from year to year so results can be compared.\n<b>Disclosure</b>: give <b>all important information</b>, including the accounting policies used, in the reports or notes.\nLink: if a business <b>does change a method</b>, it must <b>disclose</b> the change and its effect in the notes, so users can still compare.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Capital vs revenue transaction',a:'<b>Capital transaction</b>: buying or selling <b>long-term assets</b>, or long-term financing (e.g. buying a vehicle, a mortgage, owner\'s capital). It affects the Statement of Financial Position.\n<b>Revenue transaction</b>: day-to-day <b>income and expenses</b> of running the business (e.g. sales, paying wages or rent). It affects the Statement of Financial Performance.'}
]},
{t:'Section 3 – Accounting process (32 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Input, process and output of the accounting cycle',a:'Input: <b>source documents</b> (invoices, receipts)\nProcess: <b>journals and ledger</b> (recording, posting, trial balance)\nOutput: <b>financial statements / reports</b>'},
{n:'2(a).',q:'Describe events A–E',a:'A (Bank −3000, Equity −3000): an owner <b>took $3000 cash for personal use</b> (drawings).\nB (Bank +200, AR −250, Equity −50): a client <b>paid $200 to settle a $250 account</b>, and was allowed a <b>$50 discount</b> (discount allowed, an expense).\nC (Office supplies +2500, AP +2500): <b>bought office supplies on credit</b> for $2500.\nD (Bank +1250, Equity +1250): an owner <b>contributed a further $1250 cash</b>.\nE (Bank +30 000, Loan +30 000): the firm <b>borrowed $30 000 from the bank</b>.'},
{n:'2(b).',q:'Source documents for A, C, D and E',a:'A: <b>cheque butt / memo</b> (or withdrawal record)\nC: <b>supplier\'s tax invoice</b>\nD: <b>receipt / bank deposit slip</b>\nE: <b>loan agreement</b> (and bank statement)'},
{n:'3.',q:'Correct the bank account',a:'Apr 1 Balance: 2000 Dr\n4 Wages (Cr 500): 1500 Dr\n7 Sales (Dr 2000): 3500 Dr\n12 Purchases (Cr 1600): 1900 Dr\n15 Rent (Cr 1000): 900 Dr\n20 Telephone (Cr 150): <b>750 Dr</b>\n22 Loan repaid (Cr 2000): <b>1250 Cr</b>\n25 Drawings (Cr 600): <b>1850 Cr</b>\n29 Sales (Dr 5000): <b>3150 Dr</b>\nReceipts go on the debit side and payments on the credit side.\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives a final balance of 3250 Dr, but 5000 − 1850 = <b>3150 Dr</b>.'},
{n:'4(a).',q:'Insurance $1600 paid 1 Oct 2023 (year ends 31 March)',a:'Used: Oct–Mar = 6 months = $800. Prepaid: Apr–Sep = <b>$800</b>.\nDr Prepaid insurance 800\n   Cr Insurance 800'},
{n:'4(b).',q:'AR $50 000; allowance $800; new allowance 5%',a:'Needed: 5% × 50 000 = 2500. Increase: 2500 − 800 = <b>1700</b>\nDr Doubtful debts 1700\n   Cr Allowance for doubtful debts 1700'},
{n:'4(c).',q:'Commission due but not received $720',a:'Dr Accrued commission (accrued revenue) 720\n   Cr Commission income 720'},
{n:'5.',q:'Reversing a prepayment adjustment',a:'<b>Dr the expense account</b> (e.g. Insurance)\n<b>Cr Prepayment</b> (e.g. Prepaid insurance), on the first day of the new period.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Need for closing entries',a:'Closing entries <b>transfer the balances of temporary accounts</b> (revenue, expenses, drawings) to the Profit and Loss summary and then to capital. This <b>resets them to zero</b>, so the next period starts fresh and its profit only includes that period\'s income and expenses.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4 – Accounting reports (56 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Trebor Roberts: classified Statement of Financial Position at 31 Dec 2022',a:'<b>Workings</b>\nAR: 123 625 − bad debts 10 350 = 113 275; allowance 3% = 3398\nPrepaid insurance: 9/12 × 5520 = 4140 (expense 1380)\nInterest due: 7.5% × 483 000 = 36 225 − 11 500 paid = 24 725\nDepreciation 5% DV: fittings (115 000 − 11 500) × 5% = 5175; vehicle (184 000 − 18 400) × 5% = 8280; equipment (345 000 − 34 500) × 5% = 15 525\n\n<b>Current assets</b>: AR 113 275 − allowance 3398 = 109 877; petty cash 1150; inventory 5750; prepaid insurance 4140 → <b>120 917</b>\n<b>Current liabilities</b>: bank overdraft 34 500; GST 35 500; interest owing 24 725 → <b>94 725</b>\n<b>Working capital</b> = 26 192\n<b>Non-current assets</b>:\nShop fittings 115 000 − 16 675 = 98 325\nVehicle 184 000 − 26 680 = 157 320\nEquipment 345 000 − 50 025 = 294 975\nLand and buildings 869 400\nTotal <b>1 420 020</b>\nTotal assets less current liabilities = 1 446 212\nLess mortgage 483 000 → <b>Net assets 963 212</b>\n<b>Equity</b>: capital 862 500 + profit 169 712 − drawings 69 000 = <b>963 212</b>\n⚠ The paper says the net profit is $170 862, but the trial balance actually gives <b>$169 712</b>. The MOE scheme made it balance by changing GST to 34 350 (the trial balance says 35 500). If you use $170 862, your statement will be out by $1150.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Administrative expenses (extract)',a:'Insurance 1380\nBad debts 21 850 (11 500 + 10 350)\nDoubtful debts 3398\nDiscount allowed 17 250\nGeneral expenses 34 500\nOffice salaries 141 450\nRent 6900\nDepreciation – equipment 15 525\n<b>Total 242 253</b>\n(The MOE answer leaves out doubtful debts, giving 238 855. Some teachers put discount allowed under financial expenses.)'},
{n:'3.',q:'Mr BM: Statement of cash flows for 2023',a:'<b>Receipts</b>\nRent received 165 625\nCash sales (30% × 848 530) 254 559\nFrom accounts receivable 529 576\nTotal <b>949 760</b>\n<b>Payments</b>\nAdvertising 26 500\nDelivery 19 875\nCash drawings (60% × 79 500) 47 700\nGeneral expenses 39 750\nInsurance 6360\nPurchases 291 500\nRent 7950\nSalaries 162 975\nWages 106 000\nMortgage repaid (balancing figure) 285 900\nTotal <b>994 510</b>\nNet decrease in cash = 949 760 − 994 510 = <b>(44 750)</b>\nOpening bank: 5000 (in funds)\nClosing bank: <b>(39 750) overdrawn</b>, which agrees with the bank overdraft\nLeave out the non-cash items: depreciation, bad debts, discounts.\n(The MOE scheme shows the total payments as 994 540, which is a typo.)'},
{n:'4.',q:'Three limitations of financial statements',a:'1. They use <b>estimates</b> (depreciation, doubtful debts).\n2. They use <b>historical cost</b> and ignore inflation and current values.\n3. They leave out <b>non-financial</b> information (staff skill, customer loyalty, the environment), and they are about the past, not the future.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Tmk Craft Club: two main income sources',a:'<b>Sales of craft work</b> and <b>commissions</b>.'},
{n:'B2.',q:'Bank balance at 31 Dec 2023',a:'Receipts = 25 000 + 6200 + 3000 + 100 = 34 300\nPayments = 6000 + 500 + 1900 + 9000 + 6000 + 300 + 1800 + 450 = 25 950\nBank = 3200 + 34 300 − 25 950 = <b>$11 550</b>'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Craft trading statement',a:'Sales 25 000\nLess cost of sales:\nOpening inventory 2200\n+ Purchases 11 200 (9000 paid + 2200 owing)\n= 13 400\n− Closing inventory 2600\n= Cost of sales <b>10 800</b>\n<b>Gross profit 14 200</b>\nLess fair expenses:\nHall hire 5900 (6000 − 100 prepaid)\nAdvertising 500\nTransport 1800\nDepreciation of trestles 600\nTotal 8800\n<b>Profit from craft fairs 5400</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme leaves out the $2200 owed for purchases and the trestle depreciation. The question says both apply to the fairs.'},
{n:'B4.',q:'Classify the accounts',a:'Hall hire prepaid: <b>current asset</b>\nAccumulated fund: <b>capital</b>\nTrestles: <b>fixed asset</b>\nSubscriptions in arrears: <b>current asset</b>\nMortgage: <b>non-current liability</b>\nCraft purchases on credit (owing): <b>current liability</b>\nCash at bank: <b>current asset</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section 5 – Analysis and decisions (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Expense and profit percentages',a:'Selling expense % (2022) = 11 200/200 000 × 100 = <b>5.6%</b>\nAdmin expense % (2022) = (2200 + 1400 + 3800 + 2200 + 110 000 + 5200) = 124 800/200 000 × 100 = <b>62.4%</b>\nFinancial expense % (2023) = 12 500/250 000 × 100 = <b>5%</b>\nNet profit % (2023) = 60 000/250 000 × 100 = <b>24%</b>'},
{n:'2.',q:'Purpose of analysing financial information',a:'To judge the business\'s <b>profitability, liquidity and financial stability</b>, compare it with past years or other firms, find strengths and problems, and help owners, managers, lenders and investors <b>make decisions</b>.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Liquid ratio 0.90 : 1',a:'For every <b>$1 of current liabilities</b> the business has only <b>90 cents of liquid assets</b> (current assets minus inventory and prepayments). It may <b>struggle to pay its debts as they fall due</b>. This is unfavourable, because the guideline is at least 1 : 1.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Two measures each of management effectiveness and earning capacity',a:'(a) Management effectiveness: <b>inventory turnover</b>, <b>accounts receivable turnover</b> (age of debtors), expense ratios.\n(b) Earning capacity: <b>gross profit %</b>, <b>net profit %</b>, return on equity, return on assets, mark-up %.'}
]},
{t:'Section 6 – Accounting systems (56 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Two objectives of internal control',a:'Any two: <b>safeguard assets</b> (prevent theft); ensure <b>accurate, reliable records</b>; promote efficiency; make sure management policies are followed.'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Authorisation; separation of duties',a:'(a) <b>Authorisation</b>: transactions must be <b>approved by a responsible person</b> before they happen (e.g. a manager signs a purchase order), which stops unauthorised or fraudulent spending.\n(b) <b>Separation of duties</b>: <b>different people</b> do different parts of a transaction (e.g. one person receives cash, another records it, a third does the bank reconciliation). No single person can steal and hide it.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Two procedures for cash payments',a:'Any two: pay by <b>cheque or EFT</b>, not cash; payments must be <b>authorised</b> with supporting invoices; two signatures on cheques; use pre-numbered cheques; do a monthly bank reconciliation.'},
{n:'B2(a).',q:'Bank account in the ledger (May)',a:'Balance 1900 Dr\nReceipts (CRJ) Dr 3250 → 5150 Dr\nPayments (CPJ) Cr 2925 → 2225 Dr\nInsurance (bank charge not recorded) Cr 90 → <b>2135 Dr</b>'},
{n:'B2(b).',q:'Bank reconciliation at 31 May',a:'Balance per bank statement 2380 Cr\nAdd deposit not yet credited 180 → 2560\nLess unpresented cheques: #312 $75, #314 $120, #315 $230 = 425\nBalance per bank account = <b>2135 Dr</b> ✓ (agrees with the ledger)'},
{n:'C1.',q:'Perpetual inventory; reorder point',a:'(a) <b>Perpetual system</b>: the inventory record is <b>updated after every purchase and sale</b>, so the quantity and cost on hand are always known.\n(b) <b>Reorder point</b>: the stock level at which a <b>new order must be placed</b>, so the new stock arrives before the shop runs out.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'FIFO inventory card for microwaves',a:'Jun 1 Balance: 5 @ $160 = 800\nJun 5 Sale: OUT 3 @ 160 = 480 → balance 2 @ 160 = 320\nJun 12 Purchase: IN 5 @ 180 = 900 → balance 2 @ 160 + 5 @ 180 = 1220\nJun 14 <b>Return to supplier</b> (purchase return) of 1 opening-stock unit: OUT 1 @ 160 = 160 → balance 1 @ 160 + 5 @ 180 = 1060\nJun 20 Sale of 4: OUT 1 @ 160 + 3 @ 180 = 700 → balance 2 @ 180 = 360\nJun 29 Drawings: OUT 1 @ 180 = 180 → <b>balance 1 @ 180 = $180</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme treats 14 June as a sales return (adding stock back), which is wrong: the unit went back <i>to the supplier</i>, so stock goes down.'},
{n:'D1.',q:'Two controls each for accounts receivable and accounts payable',a:'AR: <b>credit checks and credit limits</b> before selling on credit; pre-numbered invoices; send monthly statements; age the debtors and follow up overdue accounts.\nAP: <b>check invoices against orders and goods received</b> before paying; payments authorised by a manager; pre-numbered cheques; reconcile with supplier statements.'},
{n:'D2.',q:'Conservatism and doubtful debts',a:'<b>Conservatism</b>: do not overstate assets or profit; recognise likely losses early. Some debtors will probably never pay, so the business creates an <b>allowance for doubtful debts</b>. This <b>reduces the value of accounts receivable</b> and the profit to a more realistic, cautious figure.'},
{n:'D3.',q:'Need for an accounts payable subsidiary ledger',a:'It keeps a <b>separate account for each supplier</b>, showing what is owed to each one and when it is due. It helps the business pay the right amounts on time and check supplier statements. Its total must <b>agree with the AP control account</b> in the general ledger.'},
{n:'E1.',q:'One control for buying fixed assets',a:'Purchases must be <b>authorised by management</b> (with quotes from several suppliers). Record each asset in a <b>fixed asset register</b>.'},
{n:'E2.',q:'Purpose of depreciation',a:'To <b>spread the cost</b> of an asset over the years it is used (matching concept), as an expense each year. It shows that the asset wears out, so the asset\'s value and the profit are not overstated.'},
{n:'E3(a).',q:'Minivan $30 000, 15% diminishing value, 4 years',a:'Insurance and registration ($800) are <b>expenses</b>, not part of the cost.\nYear 1: 30 000 → dep 4500 → acc 4500 → <b>25 500</b>\nYear 2: 25 500 → dep 3825 → acc 8325 → <b>21 675</b>\nYear 3: 21 675 → dep 3251 → acc 11 576 → <b>18 424</b>\nYear 4: 18 424 → dep 2764 → acc 14 340 → <b>15 660</b>'},
{n:'E3(b).',q:'Journal for year 1 depreciation',a:'31 Mar 2024\nDr Depreciation – vehicle 4500\n   Cr Accumulated depreciation – vehicle 4500'},
{n:'F1.',q:'Paying wages by cash, cheque and direct credit',a:'<b>Cash</b>: the money for each worker is counted into pay envelopes, and each worker signs for theirs.\n<b>Cheque</b>: a cheque is written to each employee, who banks it.\n<b>Direct credit</b>: the business tells the bank to <b>transfer the net pay straight into each employee\'s bank account</b>. This is safest and quickest.'},
{n:'F2.',q:'Importance of time sheets',a:'A <b>time sheet</b> is a record of the hours each employee works. It shows the employee\'s name, date, start and finish times, breaks, ordinary hours and overtime, and the supervisor\'s signature.\nIt is important because it is the <b>source document for calculating gross pay</b>, so workers are paid only for hours actually worked. A supervisor signing it <b>prevents fraud</b> (claiming hours not worked) and errors, and it supports overtime claims.'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/ACCOUNTING EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the scheme\'s many slips. These solutions use the figures printed in the exam paper.',
secs:[
{t:'Section 1 – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–5',q:'Correcting capitalised repairs; equation; patents; finance sources; disclosure',a:'1 <b>C</b> Dr Repairs, Cr Machinery · 2 <b>C</b> A = L + OE · 3 <b>B</b> intangible assets\n4 <b>B</b> clubs and societies do not finance businesses\n5 <b>B</b> disclosure\n⚠ The MOE key gives 4 D and 5 D, which are wrong.'},
{n:'6–10',q:'Closing inventory entry; current 3 : 1 vs liquid 0.8 : 1; net profit; invoice received; turnover 55 times',a:'6 <b>A</b> Dr Inventories, Cr Trading\n7 <b>C</b> the big gap between the current and liquid ratios means too much stock → check stock turnover\n8 <b>B</b> NP% = 55 − 14 − 18 − 8 = 15% → 15% × 7600 = $1140\n9 <b>C</b> Tio\'s Fashion Store <i>receives</i> the invoice → Purchases Journal\n10 <b>D</b> 300 ÷ 55 = 5.5 days on average\n⚠ The MOE key gives 7 A, 9 B and 10 A, which are wrong.'}
]},
{t:'Section 2 – Concepts', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Users: creditors, IRD; capital or revenue',a:'1(i) Creditors want to know if the business can <b>pay its debts</b> when due (liquidity) before giving credit.\n(ii) IRD uses the <b>net profit</b> to assess the correct income tax.\n2. New plumbing equipment – <b>capital</b>; replacing two tyres – <b>revenue</b> (maintenance); import duty on equipment – <b>capital</b> (part of cost); annual licence fee – <b>revenue</b>.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Accounting period & matching; monetary & historical cost',a:'(a) The life of the business is divided into equal <b>accounting periods</b> (usually a year). The <b>matching principle</b> then records in each period the revenue earned and the expenses incurred to earn it, so a fair profit for that period can be measured. Matching depends on having a defined period.\n(b) The <b>monetary</b> concept says only items that can be measured in money are recorded, in one currency. <b>Historical cost</b> says that money amount is the original cost at the date of the transaction. Together: transactions are recorded in dollars at their original cost.'}
]},
{t:'Section 3 – Accounting process', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Taia Ongotao: effects on the accounting equation',a:'a. Lease $3000: Bank −3000; OE −3000 (expense)\nb. Roote settles $250 debt with $200 + $50 discount: Bank +200; AR −250; OE −50\nc. Office supplies on credit: Office supplies +2500; AP +2500\nd. Cash for services: Bank +1250; OE +1250 (revenue)\ne. DBK loan: Bank +30 000; Loan +30 000'},
{n:'2a–b.',q:'Source documents and journals (Aug 2, 4, 9)',a:'Aug 2: source = <b>invoice</b> (copy sent to F Dunn); journal = <b>General Journal</b> (credit sale of a non-current asset is not a sale of inventory).\nAug 4: <b>cash register tape</b>; Cash Receipts Journal.\nAug 9: <b>receipt</b> (rent) and <b>cheque butt</b> (advertising); Cash Receipts and Cash Payments Journals.\n⚠ The MOE scheme puts Aug 2 in the Sales Journal, which is wrong.'},
{n:'2c.',q:'Cash Receipts and Cash Payments Journals',a:'<b>Cash Receipts Journal</b>\nAug 4 Sales 675\nAug 9 Rent revenue 1 260\nAug 12 Sales 1 500\nTotal bank = <b>$3 435</b> (Sales column 2 175; Sundry 1 260)\n\n<b>Cash Payments Journal</b>\nAug 9 Advertising 420\nAug 10 Stationery 890; Purchases 1 000\nTotal bank = <b>$2 310</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme uses amounts that are not in the question (2000, 500, 300, 800), which is wrong.'},
{n:'3–4.',q:'Closing entries; double entry',a:'3. Mar 31: Dr Capital 13 750 / Cr Drawings 13 750\nMar 31: Dr Profit and Loss 5 000 / Cr Advertising 5 000\n4. Every transaction affects at least two accounts: total debits always equal total credits, so the accounting equation stays balanced.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4A – Sunrise Trading and LMTA cash flow', qs:[
{n:'a.',q:'Cost of goods sold',a:'Opening inventory 15 500\n+ Purchases 89 310 − returns 110 = 89 200\n+ Customs duty 50 + Freight inwards 450\n= Goods available 105 200\n− Closing inventory 21 000\n<b>COGS = $84 200</b>'},
{n:'b.',q:'Classify expenses',a:'Selling & distribution: advertising 750, sales wages 12 600, depreciation – motor vehicle 2 520.\nAdministrative: electricity 1 500, telephone 1 250, insurance 1 350, depreciation – buildings 2 500.\nFinancial: bad debts 650 (350 + 300), discount allowed 530, interest 350.'},
{n:'c.',q:'Statement of Financial Position',a:'<b>Owner\'s equity</b>: Capital 21 380 + Net profit 62 250 − Drawings 7 650 = <b>75 980</b>\n<b>Current assets</b>: Bank 900; AR 10 100 − 300 = 9 800; Petty cash 100; Inventory 21 000 = 31 800\n<b>Current liabilities</b>: AP 8 500 → Working capital <b>23 300</b>\n<b>Fixed assets</b>: Land 30 000; Buildings 25 000 − (2 500 + 2 500) = 20 000; Motor vehicle 28 000 − (2 800 + 2 520) = 22 680 → 72 680\n(MV depreciation = 10% × (28 000 − 2 800) = 2 520)\n<b>Intangible</b>: Goodwill 10 000\nTotal = 23 300 + 72 680 + 10 000 = 105 980 − Loan (due 2025) 30 000 = <b>75 980</b> ✔ balances\n⚠ The MOE scheme writes AR as 10 000 − 300, so its working capital (23 200) is $100 short and it does not balance.'},
{n:'d.',q:'LMTA Motors statement of cash flows',a:'Receipts: Cash sales 52 000; AR 45 000; Commission 15 000; Furniture sold 10 000; Capital contributed 35 000 = <b>157 000</b>\nPayments: Cash purchases 21 000; AP 31 000; Wages 32 000; Expenses 25 000; Drawings 16 000 = <b>125 000</b>\nNet increase = <b>32 000</b>; + opening 12 500 → closing bank <b>$44 500</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme uses 50 000 sales and 25 000 capital; these differ from the question, so its figures are wrong.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4B – Betio Youth Health Club', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Main income; bank balance; health food profit',a:'1. <b>Subscriptions</b> from members.\n2. 855 + 6 388 − 2 404 = <b>$4 839</b>\n(⚠ The MOE scheme uses receipts of 4 388, giving $2 839; the paper says 6 388.)\n3. COGS = 350 + 1 450 − 450 = 1 350 → profit = 2 128 − 1 350 = <b>$778</b>'},
{n:'4–6.',q:'Equipment book value; working capital; subscriptions income',a:'4. Depreciation 10% × 3 000 = 300 → accumulated 1 300 → book value = 3 000 − 1 300 = <b>$1 700</b>\n5. CA = bank 4 839 + inventory 450 + subs in arrears 300 = 5 589; CL = subs in advance 150 → WC = <b>$5 439</b>\n6. Subscriptions = 840 − 200 (opening arrears) + 100 (opening advance) + 300 (closing arrears) − 150 (closing advance) = <b>$890</b>\n(The scheme uses $850 received, giving $900.)'}
]},
{t:'Section 5 – Analysis', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'2022: mark-up, net profit %, debtors\' turnover in days',a:'COGS = 95 400 − 58 200 = 37 200\nMark-up = GP ÷ COGS × 100 = 58 200/37 200 × 100 = <b>156.45%</b>\nNet profit % = NP ÷ Sales × 100 = 12 000/95 400 × 100 = <b>12.58%</b>\nCredit sales = 45% × 95 400 = 42 930; average AR = (12 000 + 15 000)/2 = 13 500\nDebtors\' days = 13 500/42 930 × 300 = <b>94.34 days</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme uses NP = 22 000 ÷ COGS, total (not average) debtors and 47% credit sales, so all of its figures are wrong.'},
{n:'2–3.',q:'Meaning; effects on working capital ratio',a:'2. On average it takes about <b>94 days</b> to collect cash from credit customers.\n3. a) Drawings increase → <b>decrease</b> (cash goes out). b) Long-term loan received → <b>increase</b> (cash in, no current liability).'}
]},
{t:'Section 6 – Sub-systems', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Three principles of internal control',a:'<b>Separation of duties</b> – different people handle, record and check an asset, so one person can\'t steal and hide it.\n<b>Authorisation</b> – transactions (payments, overtime) must be approved by a responsible person.\n<b>Verification/independent checks</b> – records are checked (e.g. bank reconciliations, stocktakes) to find errors.\n(Also: reliable personnel, physical security of assets, pre-numbered documents.)'},
{n:'B.',q:'Truck $35 000 + body $5 000 (insurance $500 is an expense); 20% DV',a:'1. Depreciation = <b>book value × rate</b>.\nCost = 35 000 + 5 000 = <b>40 000</b>\nYear to 31/3/22: 40 000 × 20% = 8 000 → BV 32 000\nYear to 31/3/23: 32 000 × 20% = 6 400 → BV 25 600\nYear to 31/3/24: 25 600 × 20% = 5 120\n2. 31/3/2023: Dr Depreciation – truck 6 400 / Cr Accumulated depreciation – truck 6 400\n3. Accumulated depreciation = 8 000 + 6 400 + 5 120 = <b>$19 520</b>\n4. Truck 40 000 less accumulated depreciation 19 520 = <b>20 480</b>'},
{n:'C.',q:'Cash controls; cash flow vs SFP; bank reconciliation',a:'1. Issue pre-numbered receipts for all cash; bank all takings intact daily (also separate receiving and recording).\n2. The closing balance in the statement of cash flows (opening + inflows − outflows) must equal the cash at bank shown in the SFP on the same date.\n3. Debit balance on the bank statement = <b>overdraft</b> $2 555.00\n+ Unpresented cheques (354.20 + 126.00 + 225.50) 705.70 → 3 260.70\n− Deposits not credited 1 375.00\n= Balance per cash book <b>$1 885.70 overdraft (credit)</b>'},
{n:'D.',q:'AR procedures; adjustments; creditors\' ledgers',a:'(i) Approve credit limits before selling; send monthly statements and follow up overdue accounts (also: separate recording from cash handling).\n(ii) Dr Bad debts 250 / Cr Accounts receivable 250\nNew AR 4 250 × 2% = 85; old provision 110 → reduce by 25:\nDr Provision for doubtful debts 25 / Cr Doubtful debts (P&L) 25\n(iii) Bakoa W: 1/8 bal 55 Cr; 4/8 Purchases Cr 450 → 505; 15/8 Purchase returns Dr 50 → <b>455 Cr</b>\nOnauti K: 1/8 bal 125 Cr; 10/8 Bank Dr 110, Discount received Dr 15 → <b>nil</b>\nUrua R: 1/8 bal 45 Cr; 30/8 Bank Dr 45 → <b>nil</b>\nAwata I: 19/8 Computer Cr 1 800 → <b>1 800 Cr</b>\n(Aug 6 cash purchase does not go in a creditor\'s account.)'},
{n:'E.',q:'Inventory controls; reasons stock card differs; FIFO card',a:'1. Keep stock locked/secure with limited access; regular stocktakes (also store near receiving, rotate stock).\n2. Theft or damage not recorded; counting or recording errors.\n3. FIFO perpetual:\n1/8 Balance 12 @ 15 = 180\n2/8 In 8 @ 17 = 136 → bal 12 @ 15, 8 @ 17 (316)\n3/8 Out 12 @ 15 = 180 + 2 @ 17 = 34 (214) → bal 6 @ 17 = 102\n5/8 In 6 @ 16 = 96 → bal 6 @ 17, 6 @ 16 (198)\n6/8 Out 5 @ 17 = 85 → bal 1 @ 17, 6 @ 16 = <b>$113</b>'},
{n:'F.',q:'Payroll',a:'(i) Time card / time sheet (or pay slip).\n(ii) Wages are a large cash payment; controls stop fraud (ghost employees, false hours), ensure correct pay and that tax/KPF are paid as the law requires.\n(iii) Rere Ba: rate = 350 ÷ 7 = $50/h; 1½ × 3 × 50 = 225; 2 × 3 × 50 = 300 → gross 875; deductions 25 + 20 + 85 = 130 → net <b>$745</b>\nAiwa Baba: rate = 200 ÷ 8 = $25/h; 1½ × 2 × 25 = 75; 2 × 2 × 25 = 100 → gross 375; deductions 25 + 25 + 110 = 160 → net <b>$215</b>'}
]}
]},
{y:2022, src:'P2022/KSSC Accounting 2022.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the places where the scheme is wrong.',
secs:[
{t:'Section 1 – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–10',q:'',a:'1 <b>D</b> · 2 <b>C</b> · 3 <b>A</b>\n4 <b>A</b> CA = 500 000 − 400 000 = 100 000; 100 000 : 40 000 = 2.5 : 1\n5 <b>D</b> 140 000 = L + 100 000 + 63 000 − 45 000 − 20 000 → L = $42 000\n6 <b>C</b> · 7 <b>B</b> a credit sale goes in the Sales Journal\n8 <b>B</b> net = 90% of gross → 270 ÷ 0.9 = $300\n9 <b>A</b> · 10 <b>D</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section 2 – Concepts', qs:[
{n:'1–4.',q:'Consistency; accounting period; monetary; comparability; users; uses of funds',a:'1a. <b>Consistency</b>: use the same accounting methods from period to period (e.g. same depreciation method) unless there is good reason to change and it is disclosed.\nb. <b>Accounting period</b>: the life of the business is divided into equal periods (usually 12 months) for reporting profit.\nc. <b>Monetary</b>: only transactions measurable in money are recorded, in one currency.\n2. <b>Comparability</b>: information is prepared in a way that lets users compare it with other periods and other businesses.\n3. Owners – want to know profit, return on their investment and whether the business is growing. Bank – wants to know whether the business can repay loans and interest (liquidity, security, profit).\n4. <b>Assets</b> (uses of funds); liabilities and equity are sources of funds.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Capital vs revenue expenditure',a:'Capital expenditure buys or improves non-current assets that benefit <b>more than one period</b>; it is recorded as an asset (e.g. buying a delivery van, installation costs).\nRevenue expenditure is used up within the <b>current period</b> to run the business; it is an expense in the Statement of Financial Performance (e.g. wages, repairs, vehicle registration).\nDifference: capital is non-recurring and long-term and goes to the SFP; revenue is recurring and short-term and goes to the income statement.'}
]},
{t:'Section 3 – Accounting process', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Describe each transaction',a:'a. Wrote off a bad debt of $70.\nb. Bought inventory of $750 on credit.\nc. Bought equipment of $2000, paying a $500 deposit and owing $1500.\nd. A debtor settled a $220 account by paying $200 cash; $20 discount was allowed.'},
{n:'2–3.',q:'Advantages of a computer system; source documents',a:'2. Faster processing; more accurate (automatic calculations and posting); reports produced instantly; easy to store, back up and search large amounts of data (also: fewer staff needed).\n3. a. Tax invoice (from PIN Tech) b. General journal/memo (authorised by the manager) c. Memo d. Receipt (duplicate)'},
{n:'4.',q:'Cash journals and Bank account for Ten Teuruba\'s Store',a:'<b>Cash Receipts Journal</b>: Aug 5 Sales (Nei Tio) 300 · Aug 10 Sales 550 · Aug 15 Loan – ANZ 10 000 (others) · Aug 20 Noere B 1 000 (debtors) → Bank total <b>$11 850</b> (Sales 850, Debtors 1 000, Others 10 000). (Aug 27 is a credit sale – Sales Journal.)\n<b>Cash Payments Journal</b>: Aug 1 Loan 2 000 · Aug 8 Wages 400 · Aug 22 Wages 400 · Aug 28 Drawings 200 → Bank total <b>$3 000</b> (Wages 800, Loan 2 000, Others 200).\n<b>Bank account</b>: 1/8 Balance 5 700 Dr; 31/8 Sundry receipts Dr 11 850 → 17 550 Dr; 31/8 Sundry payments Cr 3 000 → <b>14 550 Dr</b>.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Closing entries',a:'Dr Profit and Loss 1 500 / Cr Wages 1 500\nDr Trading 400 / Cr Customs duty 400\nDr Commission received 2 000 / Cr Profit and Loss 2 000\nEquipment is an <b>asset</b> – it is not closed; its balance carries forward.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4A – Nei Teiti Teboto Store', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Functions of statements; limitation',a:'1a. Shows the financial position – assets, liabilities and owner\'s equity – at a particular date.\nb. Shows cash received and paid during the period and the change from the opening to the closing bank balance.\n2. It relies on estimates (depreciation, doubtful debts) and historical cost, so profit may not be exact; it ignores non-money factors (staff skills, goodwill).'},
{n:'3.',q:'Statement of Financial Performance',a:'Sales 375 200 − returns 500 = <b>374 700</b>\nCOGS: Opening stock 15 000 + Purchases (235 500 − 300) 235 200 + Customs duty 500 − Closing stock 13 300 = <b>237 400</b>\n<b>Gross profit 137 300</b>\nOther revenue: commission 1 000 − 1 000 received in advance = <b>nil</b>\nSelling & distribution: advertising (3 300 − 300) 3 000; delivery vehicle expenses 3 500; shop wages 22 500; depreciation – vehicle (15% × 23 000) 3 450 = <b>32 450</b>\nAdministrative: electricity 12 000; insurance 6 000; office wages 15 500; depreciation – furniture & equipment (10% × 25 000) 2 500 = <b>36 000</b>\nFinancial: bad debts (250 + 250) 500; interest (1 000 + 2 000 unpaid) 3 000; discount allowed 750 = <b>4 250</b>\nTotal expenses 72 700 → <b>Net profit $64 600</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme still counts the $1 000 commission as revenue, even though all of it was received in advance, giving 65 700, which is wrong.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Classify items',a:'Loan due 2021 – <b>current liability</b>; delivery vehicle – fixed asset; accounts receivable – current asset; creditors – current liability; building – fixed asset; bank – current asset; mortgage – long-term liability; interest unpaid – current liability.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4B–C – Utiroa Soccer Club; cash flow', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Subscriptions account; per member',a:'1/8/20 Subs in arrears (Dr) 600 → 600 Dr\n1/8/20 Subs in advance (Cr) 800 → 200 Cr\n31/7/21 Bank (Cr) 4 800 → 5 000 Cr\n31/7/21 Subs in arrears c/d (Cr) 400 → 5 400 Cr\n31/7/21 Subs in advance c/d (Dr) 200 → 5 200 Cr\nTransfer to Income & Expenditure: <b>$5 200</b>\n2. 5 200 ÷ 26 = <b>$200 per member</b>'},
{n:'3–4.',q:'Accumulated fund; canteen trading statement',a:'3. Assets (1 250 + 1 150 + 600 + 10 000 − 3 000) 10 000 − liabilities (1 200 + 800) 2 000 = <b>$8 000</b>\n4. Sales 3 200; COGS = 1 150 + 1 800 − 2 220 = 730 → gross surplus 2 470; less canteen wages 600 → <b>canteen surplus $1 870</b>'},
{n:'C.',q:'Utirerei Bus Service cash flow',a:'Receipts: bus fares 2 500; accounts receivable 1 000; sale of printer 300 = <b>3 800</b>\nPayments: expenses 1 700; printer toner 700; drawings 800 = <b>3 200</b>\nNet increase = <b>600</b>; + opening 4 000 → closing bank <b>$4 600</b>\n(Gain on sale and depreciation are not cash flows.)\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives a net increase of 400 and a closing balance of 4 400. That is an arithmetic slip: 3 800 − 3 200 = 600.'}
]},
{t:'Section 5 – Analysis', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Management effectiveness; ratios; Sunrise Trading',a:'1. Stock turnover; debtors\' turnover (also: rate of return on assets/equity).\n2a. <b>Favourable</b> – the owner provides 77% of the assets, so the business relies little on borrowing and creditors.\nb. $1.25 of current assets for each $1 of current liabilities – just enough; she can pay short-term debts but with little to spare (2 : 1 is often seen as safer).\nc. Only 75c of liquid assets (no stock) per $1 of current liabilities – she could not pay her immediate debts without selling stock; aim for at least 1 : 1 by collecting debtors faster or reducing short-term debt.\n3. Mark-up = GP ÷ COGS × 100 = 66 000/44 000 × 100 = <b>150%</b>\nTotal operating expense % = 35 000/110 000 × 100 = <b>32%</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section 6 – Sub-systems', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Controls; bank reconciliation (Photos Shop)',a:'1a. Reliable personnel: employ honest, qualified, trained staff. b. Verification: records are checked by someone independent. c. Rotation of duties: staff change jobs regularly so fraud is more likely to be found.\n2a. Pay by cheque/EFT (not cash); payments authorised and supported by invoices; separate duties for approving and paying.\nb. To explain the differences between the bank statement and the cash book and find errors or fraud.\nc. Debit balance on bank statement = <b>overdraft</b> 4 300\n+ Unpresented cheques (750 + 450 + 210) 1 410 → 5 710\n− Deposits not yet credited 710\n= <b>Overdraft per cash book $5 000</b> (after the $125 dishonoured cheque is entered in the cash book).\n⚠ The MOE scheme adds the cheques as 1 400 and subtracts 2 710, which is wrong.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Inventory; weighted average card',a:'a. Inventory is a large investment and is easy to steal or damage; controls protect it and keep stock levels right.\nb. Periodic: stock is counted at the end of the period to find closing stock and COGS. Perpetual: a stock card is updated after every purchase and sale, so the balance is always known.\nc. Dec 1 Bal 86 @ $8 = 688\nDec 2 In 300 @ $10 = 3 000 → 386 @ 9.55 = 3 688\nDec 3 Out 160 @ 9.55 = 1 528.70 → 226 = 2 159.30\nDec 4 In 450 @ $12 = 5 400 → 676 @ 11.18 = 7 559.30\nDec 6 Out 330 @ 11.18 = 3 690.20 → <b>346 @ 11.18 = 3 869.10</b>'},
{n:'4.',q:'Printing machine $41 000, residual $6 000, 7 years / 150 000 units',a:'a. Dr Printing machine 41 000 / Cr Office Equipment Ltd 41 000 (insurance $1 100 is an expense).\nb. <b>Straight line</b>: (cost − residual)/life = 35 000/7 = <b>$5 000</b> a year; book value end of year 3 = 41 000 − 15 000 = <b>$26 000</b>.\n<b>Units of use</b>: (cost − residual)/total units × units used = 35 000/150 000 = $0.2333 per unit; year 3 = 25 000 × 0.2333 = <b>$5 833.33</b>; accumulated (65 000 units) = 15 166.67 → book value = <b>$25 833.33</b>.\nc. Disposal must be authorised by management; record it in the asset register and general journal (and get the best price – tenders).'},
{n:'5.',q:'Credit controls; AR control account',a:'a. Purchases only on authorised purchase orders; check goods received against order and invoice before paying.\nc. 1/8 Balance 9 500 Dr\n+ Sales 5 750; + interest charged 100\n− Cash received 3 800; − sales returns 55; − bad debts 250\n= <b>Closing balance $11 245 Dr</b>\n(Purchases, payables and interest charged by creditors belong to the AP control account.)\nd. Check customers\' credit before granting credit, with credit limits; send monthly statements and follow up overdue accounts.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Payroll',a:'1. Time cards/time sheets; pay slips (also payroll register, tax forms).\n2. Maria: 40 × 11.75 = 470.00 + 4 × 1.5 × 11.75 = 70.50 → <b>$540.50</b>\nMary: 40 × 9.15 = 366.00 + 7 × 1.5 × 9.15 = 96.08 → <b>$462.08</b>\n3. Direct credit/EFT into employees\' bank accounts – safe, quick and leaves a record (or by cheque).'}
]}
]},
{y:2021, src:'P2021/KSSC Accounting 2021.pdf', note:'Full worked solutions. No MOE marking scheme was published for this year, so every figure was worked out and checked independently.',
secs:[
{t:'Section 1 – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–10',q:'',a:'1 <b>C</b> · 2 <b>B</b> materiality · 3 <b>A</b> a credit sale goes in the Sales Journal · 4 <b>D</b> vehicle (asset) up, LMTA (liability) up\n5 <b>C</b> AR after write-off 8 000 × 2% = 160; existing 100 → increase by $60\n6 <b>B</b> (12 000 − 5 000) × 20% = $1 400\n7 <b>A</b> · 8 <b>D</b> · 9 <b>C</b> · 10 <b>D</b> obsolete stock sells slowly'}
]},
{t:'Section 2 – Concepts', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Materiality; historical cost; verifiability; understandability; entity',a:'1a. <b>Materiality</b>: items too small to affect users\' decisions can be treated in the simplest way (e.g. a $20 calculator expensed).\nb. <b>Historical cost</b>: assets are recorded at their original cost.\nc. <b>Verifiability</b>: information can be checked against evidence such as source documents.\nd. <b>Understandability</b>: reports are presented clearly so users with reasonable knowledge can understand them.\n2. The business is treated as separate from its owner, so only business transactions are recorded – this gives a true picture of the business\'s profit and position (e.g. the owner\'s house is not a business asset; goods taken are drawings).'},
{n:'3–5.',q:'Control function; capital vs revenue expenditure; going concern and accounting period',a:'3. Accounting acts as a <b>control device</b>: records let managers compare actual results with budgets and check assets, so they can find errors, waste or theft – e.g. the bank reconciliation checks cash; stock cards are checked against stocktakes.\n4. Capital expenditure buys or improves non-current assets that benefit several years (asset – e.g. buying a vehicle). Revenue expenditure is used up in the current year to run the business (expense – e.g. fuel and repairs for the vehicle).\n5. Going concern: the business is expected to continue operating in the foreseeable future. Accounting period: its life is divided into equal periods (usually a year) for reporting. Because the business is expected to continue, its life can be split into periods and the cost of long-term assets spread over them – e.g. a vehicle is depreciated over 5 years rather than expensed when bought.'}
]},
{t:'Section 3 – Processing', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Kabaki Trading',a:'a. Trans 1: <b>credit note received</b> (from Bobotin – or a debit note sent). Trans 2: <b>credit note issued</b> (copy). Trans 3: <b>memo</b>.\nb. Trans 3: General Journal. Trans 4: Cash Payments Journal. Trans 5: Sales Journal.\nc. <b>Drawings</b>.\nd. The goods were damaged/faulty, the wrong goods were sent, or they were overcharged.'},
{n:'2–3.',q:'Closing and reversing entries',a:'2a. Dr Trading 2 000 / Cr Freight inward 2 000\nb. Dr Profit and Loss 5 000 / Cr Capital 5 000\n3a. 1/1/21 Dr Rent revenue 200 / Cr Accrued rent revenue 200\nb. 1/1/21 Dr Accrued wages 100 / Cr Wages 100'},
{n:'4.',q:'Tikitiki Trading adjustments at 30 June 2020',a:'a. Interest = 12 000 × 10% ÷ 12 = $100 a month; May + June = $200 → Dr Accrued interest revenue 200 / Cr Interest revenue 200\nb. Earned Apr–Jun = 3 × 500 = 1 500; received 2 500 → $1 000 in advance → Dr Rent revenue 1 000 / Cr Rent received in advance 1 000\nc. Advertising 300 + penalty 10% = 30 → Dr Advertising 300; Dr Penalty (interest) expense 30 / Cr Accrued expenses 330\nd. (50 000 − 5 000)/50 = $900 a year; one month (June) = <b>$75</b> → Dr Depreciation – building 75 / Cr Accumulated depreciation – building 75'},
{n:'5.',q:'Need for closing entries',a:'A closing entry transfers the balance of a temporary account (revenue, expense, drawings) to the Trading/Profit and Loss or Capital account at the end of the period. This is needed so profit is measured for that period only and the revenue and expense accounts start the next period at zero (matching and accounting period concepts). E.g. wages $5 000 is closed to Profit and Loss so next year\'s wages are recorded separately.'}
]},
{t:'Section 4 – Reports', qs:[
{n:'1a.',q:'Nanomaka Store Statement of Financial Performance',a:'Sales 68 680 − returns 1 620 = <b>67 060</b>\nCOGS: Opening inventory 15 000 + purchases (25 240 − returns 2 490 − drawings 240) 22 510 + customs duty 50 + cartage inward 390 − closing inventory 20 000 = <b>17 950</b>\n<b>Gross profit 49 110</b>\nOther revenue: commission 3 670; rent (12 × 400) 4 800 = 8 470 → 57 580\nSelling: sales wages 13 400; cartage outward 950 = <b>14 350</b>\nAdministrative: office wages 25 060; insurance (12 × 80) 960 = <b>26 020</b>\nFinancial: doubtful debts (1% × 10 000 − 80) 20; loan interest (10% × 8 000) 800 = <b>820</b>\nTotal expenses 41 190 → <b>Net profit $16 390</b>'},
{n:'1b.',q:'Classify two items each',a:'Non-current assets: land, buildings (delivery vehicles). Current assets: bank, accounts receivable (inventory, accrued items). Investments: interest-bearing deposit, shares in BKL. Current liabilities: accounts payable, rent received in advance (accrued insurance $80, accrued loan interest $800).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Matoatoa Store cash position',a:'Receipts: cash sales (80% × 50 000) 40 000; accounts receivable 20 000; commission 3 000 = <b>63 000</b>\nPayments: cash purchases (70% × 75 000) 52 500; expenses (25 000 − 2 000 depreciation) 23 000 = <b>75 500</b>\nNet decrease = <b>12 500</b>; opening 15 000 → closing bank <b>$2 500 Dr</b>'},
{n:'3–5.',q:'Tarawa Boxing Club; R&P vs I&E; club income',a:'3. Income: surplus from bar 1 710; subscriptions (940 + 95 owing − 75 in advance) 960; donations 150; gate fees 400; competition fees 300 = <b>3 520</b>\nExpenses: electricity 300; rent 1 200; insurance 200 = <b>1 700</b>\n<b>Surplus $1 820</b> (the bar delivery cost is already in the bar trading surplus).\n4. (1) R&P records actual cash received and paid (including capital items); I&E records income earned and expenses incurred for the period (accrual basis, revenue items only). (2) R&P ends with a cash balance; I&E ends with a surplus or deficit.\n5. Subscriptions, entrance/gate fees, donations, fundraising/bar sales (also competition fees, sponsorship).'}
]},
{t:'Section 5 – Analysis', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Bitibiti Trading 2020',a:'a. Mark-up = GP/COGS × 100 = 100 000/130 000 × 100 = <b>76.9%</b> – up from 68%: higher selling prices or cheaper purchases; favourable.\nb. Total expense % = 85 000/230 000 × 100 = <b>37.0%</b> – up from 20%: expenses are poorly controlled; unfavourable.\nc. Average AR = (70 000 + 60 000)/2 = 65 000 → 65 000/230 000 × 365 = <b>103.2 days</b> – slower than 90 days; credit control has worsened.'},
{n:'2–3.',q:'Liquidity; ratios',a:'2. The ability of a business to pay its short-term debts when they fall due (enough cash or assets easily turned into cash).\n3. a. Accounts receivable turnover (collection period) b. Return on total assets (asset turnover) c. Equity ratio / debt ratio (financial structure) d. Inventory turnover'}
]},
{t:'Section 6 – Sub-systems', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Accounts receivable – Abiata Store',a:'a. Check customers\' credit rating and set credit limits; send monthly statements and follow up overdue accounts; separate the duties of recording sales, receiving cash and keeping the ledger (also pre-numbered invoices).\nb(i) <b>Timiti</b>: 1/6 Bal 500 Dr; 2/6 Sales 400 → 900; 10/6 Bank 500 and Discount 40 (Cr) → 360; 18/6 Sales returns 80 → <b>280 Dr</b>\n<b>Tiemiti</b>: 1/6 Bal 150; 6/6 Freight 30 → 180; 22/6 Returns 40 → 140; 25/6 Sales 100 → <b>240 Dr</b>\n(ii) <b>AR Control</b>: Bal 850 + sales 1 300 + freight 30 + interest 30 = 2 210; less bank 600, discount 40, returns 120, bad debts 100 → <b>1 350 Dr</b>\n(Check: Timiti 280 + Tiena 300 + Tiemiti 240 + Uriam 530 = 1 350 ✔)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Inventory – Sam\'s Retail Store (FIFO)',a:'a. Keep stock locked with limited access; regular stocktakes compared with stock cards; record all stock movements on authorised documents (also set reorder levels).\nb(i) Jan 1 Bal 100 @ $5 = 500\nJan 5 In 90 @ $6 = 540 → 100 @ 5, 90 @ 6 = 1 040\nJan 10 Out 100 @ $5 = 500 + 10 @ $6 = 60 (total 560) → 80 @ 6 = 480\nJan 15 In 60 @ $7 = 420 → 80 @ 6, 60 @ 7 = <b>900</b>\n(ii) Jan 10: Dr Bank 880 / Cr Sales 880 (110 × $8); Dr Cost of goods sold 560 / Cr Inventory 560'},
{n:'3.',q:'Fixed assets – vehicle $15 000, 25% DV, bought 1 June 2018',a:'a. Authorise purchases and disposals; keep a fixed asset register with ID numbers; physically check assets regularly; insure and secure them.\nb. (part-year for 2018 – 7 months)\n31/12/18: 15 000 × 25% × 7/12 = 2 187.50 → accumulated 2 187.50 → carrying 12 812.50\n31/12/19: 12 812.50 × 25% = 3 203.13 → accum 5 390.63 → carrying 9 609.37\n31/12/20: 9 609.37 × 25% = 2 402.34 → accum 7 792.97 → carrying <b>7 207.03</b>\nc. Dr Disposal 15 000 / Cr Vehicle 15 000\nDr Accumulated depreciation 7 792.97 / Cr Disposal 7 792.97\nDr Bank 5 000 / Cr Disposal 5 000\nDr Loss on sale 2 207.03 / Cr Disposal 2 207.03\n(If a full year is charged in 2018: carrying amounts 11 250, 8 437.50, 6 328.12 and the loss is 1 328.12.)'},
{n:'4.',q:'Collecting hours worked',a:'Time cards with a time clock; timesheets signed by a supervisor; electronic swipe cards or fingerprint (biometric) readers.'}
]}
]}
]});
